SM(177,{"n":"1 Peter 3","a":"Fr Stephen De Young Archives/1 Peter 3.mp3","d":3279.4,"s":[[0.32,"Come and study the Holy Scriptures with us as Father Stephen DeYoung teaches verse by verse on the podcast The Whole Counsel of God."],[9.04,"Father Stephen holds a PhD in Biblical Studies, and is an Orthodox priest serving at Archangel Gabriel Orthodox Church in Lafayette, Louisiana."],[21.2,"Okay, so we'll go ahead and get started."],[24.16,"And when we get started here in just a moment, we will be picking up in the first epistle of St."],[33.28,"Peter, chapter 3, verse 1."],[40.48,"So before we do that, though, I know everyone seeing the heading just wants to dive right in."],[47.92,"But before we do that, sort of a brief recap."],[55.56,"We've seen Saint Peter, he's moved through a bunch of topics, so I'm trying to decide how how much depth I want to go into because I don't want to spend the whole night recapping."],[66.52,"But we'll just kind of do do chapter two what we're what we're up to to get us caught up right now."],[73.72,"But um so St."],[77.08,"Peter's kind of been going back and forth."],[79.72,"Um we've seen he uses therefore the way St."],[82.84,"Paul does, like he'll lay something out and then he'll say therefore and then apply it."],[87.82,"Sort of practical application."],[89.98,"But unlike St."],[91.02,"Paul, where St."],[91.9,"Paul will do that like across his whole epistle."],[95.6,"He'll in the first part of the epistle, he'll lay a whole, lay out a whole bunch of theology, then he'll say, therefore, and then it will be practical from then on."],[103.76,"St. Saint Peter has kind of switches back and forth."],[107.81,"So he'll lay something out, then sort of immediately apply it, and then lay something out, sort of immediately apply it."],[113.81,"And he's kind of chaining those together."],[116.55,"So he hasn't had just sort of one argument all the way through the way Saint Paul often would."],[122.95,"Uh, but he's he's made a a number of arguments that are sort of linked linked together."],[128.95,"So they're not completely unrelated, but it's not part of one big one big whole."],[135.65,"And chiefly in the last portion, bringing us up to where we are tonight, he's been talking about uh the uh the calling of the church of the Christian community, to be a priesthood, sort of connected to the um the way he talked about the Old Testament priesthood."],[159.05,"And so that requires of Christians holiness to be set apart."],[165.78,"All of those things that were required in the old covenant of the priesthood."],[170.76,"Of Israel as a whole to be set apart from the nations, and of the priesthood to be set apart from Israel, are now required of Christians as well."],[178.86,"So he's been talking about a number of things, both in terms of the relations of Christians to each other, that being holy and pure, but also a unity of love."],[190.7,"And then he's now shifted, and that's part of where we're gonna be picking up now"],[197.7,"Um we ended off on him talking about slavery, which is also super non-controversial."],[203.06,"Um, but um, and he's now shifted to also including how then does the Christian community relate to the world outside."],[215.27,"So there's both intern our internal relationships within the church, what's to characterize those, and then how the character our character as Christians and as a Christian community as the church is to then represent itself to those outside, and so as I mentioned when we left off, um he was talking about he started talking about in that category of the relationship between the church and the outside world, talking about within the framework of persecution and derision coming from the outside toward the church and toward Christians."],[256.61,"That the correct response to that was not to fight back, or even to try to defend ourselves or the church, but to do good."],[266.7,"R rightight,? To do good, so that when they make false accusations against Christians, they'll look foolish."],[273.79,"Because Christians will be known for doing good, practicing kindness, being Christ-like toward other people outside."],[283.4,"And he even went so far as to say, you know, even if that doesn't work out that way in this life, then at least when we stand before Christ on the day of judgment, there won't be any accusations against us that will stick or have any merit to them."],[301.35,"Um, and then from that broad idea, he's now focusing on specific and difficult cases."],[310.87,"So the first one of those had to do with slavery."],[314.37,"So there were lots of Christians who were slaves, as we talked about last time, uh, slaves."],[319.53,"Slaves and women were the most common converts to Christianity in the in the first century and most of the second century."],[330.09,"And so there were even bishops who were slaves."],[340.17,"Outside of the church, there was someone who quote unquote owned them and could do with them whatever they wanted."],[346.59,"And so St."],[349.63,"Peter applied that same principle in that relationship."],[353.56,"That as Christians, we don't participate in slave revolts, we don't try to kill this person, uh, but that we do good so that possibly in this world the person will be shamed, if they treat us poorly, barring that when we stand before Christ."],[377.11,"Then we'll be rewarded for for having done the right thing."],[381.59,"And so it's important to lay that out, that part in particular, before we get into what we're about to get into, as you all saw the heading."],[391.72,"Because it's very easy when we get to parts of the Bible."],[397.4,"We already went through some of these with St."],[399.32,"Paul. But like this in St."],[401.29,"Peter, to take this stuff about husbands and wives and men and women and pull it out of context and treat it as like, well, here's St."],[408.85,"Peter weighing in on gender theory."],[410.86,"Or something, which is not what this is."],[413.8,"So we have to take it within the overall context."],[418.84,"That's one of the perils for how we read the Bible a lot of times."],[422.44,"I've mentioned before sort of the concordance way of reading the Bible."],[427.74,"Where you say, well, I want to find out about faith, so I'm gonna find all the places that talk about faith, and I'm gonna read those, and I'm gonna."],[434.19,"We tend to do these sort of topical things."],[437.36,"And that does a lot of violence, to the to the text."],[441.53,"So we can go there."],[442.49,"I want to find everything that talks about men and women."],[444.95,"I'm just gonna pull all of those out of context and then try to construct something from them."],[448.71,"We wouldn't read any other book that way"],[453.48,"Like you wouldn't get a book about German history and say, I'm going to find all the places that talk about sausage."],[460.86,"And go through and take all the extracts that mention someone eating sausage or making sausage or the sausage industry."],[467.44,"And pull those O out and be like, ah, so this is what the author has to say about sausage."],[472.81,"That would make that would make no sense."],[474.83,"And we definitely would do that with a fiction book."],[477.81,"I'm going to go through Moby Dick and find all the passages where they're talking about the harpoons."],[482.42,"And do a study of harpoons in Moby Dick."],[486.0,"Well, you might if you have to write a dissertation."],[487.76,"Yeah, exactly. Or a journal article."],[489.12,"But other barring that."],[489.84,"And so we shouldn't read the Bible that way either"],[490.97,"You wouldn't do that."],[495.19,"So, yes, Peter is gonna say some things here about how husbands relate to wives, just like he talked about how Christian slaves should relate to slave masters and slave owners."],[505.85,"But that's within a context of something larger that he's saying."],[509.69,"And he's kind of using these things as examples of a general principle."],[514.09,"So if you don't know what that general principle is, you don't compare it with the other examples, you're not gonna be able to interpret and understand what he's saying correctly."],[525.66,"So all that's it."],[527.3,"Unless there are any questions or comments or hints or allegations or things left unsaid."],[532.7,"We'll pick up in uh first epistle of St"],[540.1,"Peter chapter 3, verse 1."],[544.18,"Non controversial. Wives likewise, be submissive to your own husbands, that even if some do not obey the word, they without a word may be won by the conduct of their wives."],[558.26,"When they observe your chaste conduct accompanied by fear?"],[567.66,"That doesn't help either."],[570.48,"Um, but so let's go through this."],[573.04,"So there are several key points."],[574.52,"So, first of all, you notice that likewise."],[578.48,"That's literally Greek in the same way."],[581.12,"In the same way as what?"],[583.52,"As the slaves."],[584.85,"That's the comparison he's making."],[587.01,"Okay. Why would he make that comparison?"],[591.09,"Because he's writing to women living in the Roman world."],[595.14,"And there's not that much difference, in terms of a woman's ability to uh self govern."],[606.61,"The husband under Roman law, especially if he's a Roman citizen, could literally have her kill."],[614.81,"If she displeases him, or just divorce her and throw her out on the street."],[619.5,"He could do whatever he wants."],[621.51,"And so. Yeah, so that's that's an important note here in the comparison."],[631.19,"So this is someone who likewise might find herself powerless, depending on what kind of man she ended up married to."],[639.59,"And also at this time in history, women weren't meeting men and falling in love and deciding to get married."],[648.0,"ere were th Those were all arranged marriages."],[650.52,"These were all arranged marriages in their teenage early teenage years."],[655.77,"Um, yes, yeah, for the women."],[660.98,"They didn't get to pick."],[662.87,"And so they could literally end up, they could end up married to someone truly horrible."],[669.72,"And there was nothing they could do about it."],[672.38,"They couldn't just go leave."],[674.44,"I mean they could run away the way a slave could run away."],[677.18,"And try and escape."],[678.14,"But I mean, barring that."],[680.24,"There wasn't like a recourse."],[681.52,"They couldn't get divorced and then go and find somebody else and marry them."],[684.89,"That that didn't gonna happen."],[687.51,"Um that's important, the comparison he's making there."],[694.71,"That they might find themselves in a similar situation."],[698.28,"Also, notice it's wives be sub.missive to your own husbands"],[704.56,"He's not saying all women in general need to be submissive toward all men in general."],[710.22,"So he's talking about within a particular relationship."],[714.64,"How a woman who is a Christian ought to conduct herself within that relationship."],[719.28,"And that even if some do not obey the word, and remember, we said word here should be capitalized."],[726.24,"This isn't talking about the Bible."],[728.16,"They don't have a Bible."],[729.89,"And the point here is that this is a person, if there's someone who doesn't obey the word, this is a person who's not a Christian."],[734.93,"So if they're not a Christian, they're definitely not reading the Bible."],[738.23,"So it should be capital W."],[739.91,"So if they're not obedient to Christ, meaning if they're not a Christian."],[743.77,"If they're not part of the Christian community."],[745.61,"You're a woman who has become a Christian, your husband's a pagan."],[750.7,"And this was very, as we said, slaves and women were the most common converts at this at this point in history."],[758.0,"And there's good reason for that."],[760.01,"Because Christianity was the only religion or philosophy going that said they were equally human."],[767.45,"Literally equally human."],[769.21,"We're not talking about like equal rights or voting or anything like that."],[772.97,"We're talking about your human."],[776.33,"The same way your husband or the Roman citizen who owns you is human."],[781.45,"The same way Caesar is human."],[783.23,"Christianity is it."],[784.43,"And so that obviously is very attractive to people who have spent their life being told they're subhuman."],[791.65,"And been being treated like animals in some cases."],[795.63,"If you go and read Greek philosophers, the Greeks were especially bad about this."],[799.95,"Sorry, Greeks. But the ancient Greeks were rabid misogynists."],[806.49,"We've probably mentioned I've probably mentioned before in this Bible study."],[809.29,"Like Aristotle thought that women were a birth defect."],[814.09,"Like that all fetuses tried to become boys, and some of them didn't make quite make it."],[819.77,"Did gel and they were born as women."],[821.62,"So that's the like we think some guy makes lewd comments that he's a misogynist."],[826.74,"Like, this is next level stuff."],[829.72,"You know, Socrates said it was impossible for a man to be in love with a woman because she was beneath him."],[836.52,"So it was not possible for them to find love."],[839.24,"That's where we're at."],[839.98,"And Socrates was married, so factor that in."],[843.64,"Um, yeah, that's that's what we're and that's why they're attracted."],[850.12,"But so that means lots of slaves and lots of women are finding themselves in this situation."],[855.48,"So what St."],[856.12,"Peter is saying is not just like a hypothetical."],[859.32,"This is a very practical thing."],[861.16,"There were lots of Christian women who found themselves married to pagan Roman men."],[865.38,"And being pagan Roman men, they didn't act like Christians."],[868.66,"They didn't put a lot of value in things like kindness and gentleness and faithfulness."],[873.14,"Those things that, you know, the fruit of the spirit"],[873.91,"And self-control. Love, joy, peace, those things that we're cultivating."],[881.43,"In fact, Roman society cultivated the exact opposite."],[886.57,"They weren't about peace."],[887.85,"I mean, the the they talk about the Pax Romana, but Libe said, you know, they've made a wasteland and called it peace."],[893.47,"You go and kill everybody, and now it's beautiful and peaceful with no humans left."],[898.41,"Um, so they value, you know, power and strength."],[904.78,"And and swift action."],[907.02,"And so all of the values of the culture that these non-Christian men were indoctrinated in, are are opposed to what? they now believe in, right"],[918.19,"And so he's saying, nonetheless, nonetheless, they need to be obedient to their husbands."],[927.78,"Not run away and flee, just like the slaves shouldn't run away and free and flee."],[932.2,"Try to keep peace in the household."],[936.49,"Why? Why should they do that?"],[939.4,"Because he thinks women should just take what they can get or don't deserve better."],[944.04,"No, that's not what he's saying."],[945.16,"He says, so that even if, they don't obey the word, they're not Christians."],[951.55,"They, without a word, without the word."],[954.13,"Apart from Christ, may be won by the conduct of their wives when they observe your chase conduct, accompanied by that fear should be like reverence."],[962.89,"That's like the fear of God kind of fear."],[965.05,"Not like when they see you're terrified of them, they'll have a change of heart."],[969.74,"That's um, so the idea here is that if you behave toward them in a Christian manner, that might win them over to Christ."],[981.44,"That's the goal."],[983.22,"That's the goal."],[984.18,"So he's saying to those women, even if your husband is a tyrant, as a Christian, your goal is not that you get free from them, not to cross your fingers that you get widowed."],[996.56,"Not to right try again, but your goal needs to be his salvation, that he would come around, and find salvation."],[1004.78,"And by the way, if he does find salvation, that'll change the dynamics of your relationship quite a bit."],[1011.38,"But that's that's the goal."],[1017.38,"Verse 3, do not let your adornment be merely outward, arranging the hair, wearing gold, or putting on fine apparel."],[1024.98,"Rather, let it be the hidden person of the heart with the incorruptible beauty of a gentle and quiet spirit, which is very precious in the sight of God."],[1034.2,"So he's he's making a comparison."],[1037.69,"He's saying, don't just try and look nice outwardly, but be concerned about virtue."],[1040.25,"Becoming like Christ, serving Christ"],[1047.55,"That's the important thing."],[1048.83,"That's the thing that's going to win him over."],[1052.35,"Not him looking at you and thinking you're really pretty."],[1059.12,"Verse 5 For in this manner in former times the holy women who trusted in God also adorned themselves, being submissive to their own husbands, as Sarah Sarah obeyed Abraham, calling him Lord, whose daughters you are, if you do good and are not afraid with any terror."],[1078.72,"So wherever he said with fear, and now he's saying you shouldn't be afraid with any terror."],[1082.85,"That's why I said the fear is like kind of a bad translation."],[1088.59,"So he's acknowledging there at the end that for them to do this, if they've got a husband who's a petty tyrant, for them to do this is going to require bravery."],[1102.37,"This is going to require courage on their part."],[1104.61,"That's one of the virtues this is going to require."],[1107.83,"And endurance and patience."],[1111.48,"And he's using Sarah and Abraham, their relationship as an example."],[1116.09,"Which wasn't always great."],[1117.53,"There was the whole him lying about her being his wife, and you know, other men trying to take her when he was in Egypt twice."],[1127.39,"There was the whole episode with Hagar."],[1130.75,"There were, I mean."],[1132.24,"So Abraham and Sarah are a good example."],[1134.5,"They didn't have like a perfect."],[1135.94,"It's not Isaac and Rebecca."],[1137.32,"Isaac and Rebecca is presented as like this perfect and beautiful loving relationship."],[1142.05,"Abraham and Sarah, a little bit rocky."],[1146.27,"Jacob and Leah and Rachel, all kinds of problematic."],[1149.31,"So he didn't use that as an example either."],[1151.49,"But Abraham and Sarah are in between."],[1153.67,"Abraham and Sarah did have this loving relationship ultimately, but there were plenty of issues."],[1160.69,"There were plenty of problems."],[1162.06,"So he's saying, be like, you know, Sarah is an example."],[1165.68,"You're gonna need to have courage and patience to do this."],[1170.01,"But notice what he how he is talking to the woman."],[1175.15,"We very easily, as modern people, get sidetracked by the word submissive."],[1179.63,"Go. But he's talking to her and encouraging her, again, as a human."],[1188.93,"He's saying this stuff particularly to women here, but we saw earlier in the epistle, he was addressing this stuff to Christians in general, men too."],[1200.58,"Men too. When you say, gird up the loins of your mind, be sober."],[1205.12,"All of these things, give it your former lusts."],[1207.18,"It was just Christians in general."],[1209.8,"Meaning he's treating these wives of pagan husbands as Christians."],[1216.25,"As humans. And this is a marked change from the old covenant."],[1225.05,"When you read the commandments in the Torah, pretty much none of them are directed at women."],[1233.07,"They don't turn around and do both."],[1234.75,"Like it doesn't say you shall not covet your neighbor's wife or husband."],[1242.45,"When you look at the commandments about sexual immorality, they're all directed at men."],[1247.89,"Including the stuff about homosexuality is only directed at men."],[1249.41,"he's only directed. All the way down at men"],[1254.97,"That's because in the late Bronze Age, even more so than in the Roman Empire."],[1261.66,"Women didn't get to make those kinds of decisions."],[1264.32,"And so they weren't held accountable for those decisions because they didn't get to make them."],[1269.77,"It was men who were being held accountable because they were the ones making all the decisions."],[1274.07,"So we see that something has shifted here."],[1277.56,"So women now in in the early church have a little not as much as today, but they have more autonomy."],[1286.46,"We saw how St."],[1287.5,"Paul said, we talked about the infamous women should be silent in church passage."],[1293.44,"And how the word for silence there is actually the word for silence that's used for like meditation."],[1300.73,"The idea being that unlike in former times when they thought women were too dumb to learn anything, and so the men folk would go and listen to the sermon in the synagogue, and the women could go back and have their coffee time chat."],[1315.55,"Because they can't understand this stuff anyway."],[1317.97,"St. Paul was saying, no, they need to learn too."],[1320.93,"They need to listen too."],[1322.61,"They need to become disciples of Christ too."],[1324.83,"So we see this shift happening."],[1326.27,"St. Peter's doing the same thing here."],[1330.05,"He's not just saying, hey, men."],[1332.93,"You need to be nicer to your wives"],[1335.66,"You need to treat your wives the way Christ treats the church."],[1338.64,"He could. I mean, that's a perfectly valid thing to say to husbands."],[1341.84,"St. Paul says basically that to husbands."],[1343.92,"He says, husbands love your wives as Christ loves the church."],[1347.97,"But he's also speaking to the wives now."],[1351.33,"Especially wives who are in this particularly difficult situation."],[1356.03,"And saying, you could do this."],[1357.87,"You can acquire these virtues just as much as a man in the church can acquire these virtues."],[1364.19,"You could also be self-controlled and patient and loving and compassionate and show courage."],[1370.53,"You can do these things too."],[1372.77,"And that means you're called to do these things too."],[1376.13,"You're now responsible to do these things too."],[1379.94,"Because this is something you can do."],[1386.66,"And so now we're going to see that verse 7, he speaks to the husbands, but notice how many verses he speaks to the husbands."],[1395.78,"One. Verse 7."],[1400.99,"Husbands likewise dwell with them with understanding, giving honor to the wife as to the weaker vessel, and as heirs together of the grace of life, that your prayers may not be hindered."],[1414.83,"So again, there's a weaker vessel!"],[1417.71,"How dare you, sir?"],[1418.99,"That phrase is such a long though one of my favorite how can I put it a way to look at it, if something is weaker that needs to be compared in a um comparable way to something such as a weak vessel."],[1441.83,"So you know there's an equality in the sense that both of us you know we are weak."],[1449.27,"And no so so it's not so much men or strong and you know we're gonna be whatever away down here."],[1460.39,"It's like ah well really are equal in um well also the vessel part is important."],[1471.88,"Yeah. We just hear weaker."],[1473.74,"We're like, well what, physically weaker?"],[1475.5,"Well, okay, on average male humans are physically stronger than female humans."],[1479.66,"That's not what it's getting at."],[1480.96,"A vessel is what?"],[1483.2,"At this time in history?"],[1484.88,"Pottery. Okay. Does weaker pottery mean it can't lift as much?"],[1492.16,"Or does it mean it's more delicate and fragile?"],[1496.32,"So if you're carrying something that's made of glass, that's fragile, how are you gonna treat it?"],[1506.8,"You're not gonna be rough with it."],[1507.76,"You're going to be gentle with it"],[1513.04,"You're going to treat it with care."],[1518.45,"And so that's more what he's aiming at."],[1522.91,"Because what is he just talked about?"],[1525.47,"He's just talked about husbands who behave kind of like petty tyrants toward their wives."],[1530.83,"They're behaving like the way a slave owner relates to a slave."],[1535.07,"That's how they're relating to their wife."],[1537.07,"Okay. So what's the opposite of that?"],[1541.64,"Treating your wife like your personal slave."],[1545.48,"The opposite of that is treating her with gentleness and with respect and with kindness and with care."],[1548.84,"Rather than being brutal to her, you're doing the exact opposite"],[1555.91,"So that's where the idea, and that equality does come out, especially that as being heirs together of the grace of life."],[1566.37,"Heirs together of the grace of life."],[1568.29,"You are co heirs."],[1571.49,"Why is that so powerful as a statement of equality?"],[1576.69,"Women generally not heirs."],[1578.45,"Exactly. Women didn't inherit things."],[1582.31,"The son was the heir."],[1583.91,"The male was the heir."],[1586.56,"He's saying you're both heirs of salvation."],[1592.45,"And so this is a quality."],[1594.93,"He's not talking about physical strength."],[1597.81,"He's not talking about roles in society."],[1600.93,"He's not talking about any of that."],[1602.39,"He's saying before Christ."],[1603.75,"The same way St."],[1604.55,"Paul said. Before Christ, there's no male or female Jew or Gentile slave or free."],[1609.03,"In terms of salvation, before Christ, you're on the same plane."],[1614.79,"And because of that, you don't treat her like a slave."],[1619.59,"You don't treat her like subhuman."],[1622.32,"You do the opposite."],[1623.36,"You hold her up and you treat her as something precious and delicate and that you care for."],[1629.82,"And why? See, he has a so that there."],[1632.14,"So that your prayers may not be hindered"],[1637.38,"What does that mean?"],[1639.06,"Well, you're gonna ask God to show mercy on you."],[1645.7,"How can you do that if you're not showing mercy to your own wife, let alone anybody else?"],[1653.4,"You're gonna ask God to show kindness and have compassion on you when you pray."],[1658.92,"How can you do that if you're not showing kindness and compassion to your wife, the closest person to you?"],[1665.21,"So St. Peter is saying, if you don't do this, then don't expect God, to treat you the way you aren't treating your wife."],[1675.68,"And this is because of, remember, we've got this outlying societal arrangement where before her husband, she's basically legally, etc. helpless."],[1685.86,"Well, in the same way, we're all kind of helpless before God."],[1690.79,"And so we need to show what we expect God to show to us."],[1696.95,"And this is a basic principle."],[1698.49,"All through the gospels that Christ comes back."],[1700.89,"You know, if we want to be forgiven, we need to forgive others."],[1705.58,"And so he's saying that's especially intense when we're talking about the closest person, the closest other human to you."],[1713.67,"So don't expect your prayers to go further than the ceiling if you're not practicing what you expect to receive from God."],[1730.27,"Verse 8, finally, this is St."],[1734.27,"Paul did this a few times too."],[1735.71,"This is like the dead center of this epistle."],[1738.03,"And he says, Finally, sort of like, let us complete our prayer to the Lord with about 45 minutes left in uh liturgy."],[1746.59,"Uh finally, all of you be of one mind, having compassion for one another."],[1752.19,"Love as brothers, be tender hearted, be courteous."],[1756.29,"So he's now extending what he just said to husbands and wives, to the way everyone should act toward each other in the church."],[1764.51,"So this is how things should be in a marriage."],[1766.43,"This is also how things should be within the whole Christian community and how we behave for each other."],[1772.43,"Not returning evil for evil or reviling for reviling, but on the contrary, blessing, knowing that you are called to this, that you may inherit a blessing."],[1784.05,"So this is that same principle again."],[1787.43,"There are going to be people, even in the church, who are occasionally going to do evil things to you."],[1792.71,"And reviling, who are occasionally going to say evil things to you."],[1797.51,"And when that happens, we have a choice."],[1800.64,"We can hit back."],[1801.84,"And what is what does St"],[1802.81,"Or we can bless in response."],[1806.81,"Peter say? He said, We're here, we're Christians, we're serving the Lord because we expect to inherit a blessing."],[1814.41,"So if we expect to inherit a blessing, if we want the blessings of God, then what do we need to be doing to other people?"],[1821.85,"We need to be blessing them, even if they're not blessing us."],[1827.66,"That's the idea."],[1828.78,"What we want to receive from God, we need to be giving to others."],[1833.12,"Because guess what?"],[1834.56,"We're not always that great toward God, and we still want him to bless us."],[1843.33,"Verse 10, for he who would love life and see good days, let him refrain his tongue from evil and his lips from speaking deceit."],[1852.81,"Let him turn away from evil and do good."],[1855.45,"Let him seek peace and pursue it."],[1857.61,"For the eyes of the Lord are on the righteous, and his ears are open to their prayers, but the face of the Lord is against those who do evil."],[1867.61,"So that's uh from Psalm 34 or uh 33 in the Greek."],[1874.57,"So um, and he quotes it at length."],[1878.13,"But this is basically what he's been saying the whole time."],[1881.25,"He's been applying it to specific situations."],[1883.7,"But this sort of ties it all together."],[1886.92,"If you want to have a good life, you want to have a long life."],[1890.94,"Then these are the things you need to do."],[1893.9,"Keep your tongue from evil, your lips from speaking deceit, turn away from evil and do good."],[1898.8,"Repent, seek peace and pursue it."],[1901.41,"And then God's eyes will be upon you and he will hear your prayers."],[1907.01,"But the face of the Lord is against those who do evil."],[1915.97,"So verse 13, now he's going to segue a little bit."],[1924.29,"And who is he who will harm you if you become followers of what is good?"],[1928.93,"So he's now segueing from that."],[1930.41,"So if you're doing this, you're pursuing peace, you're pursuing good."],[1935.15,"You're speaking the truth and speaking speaking well, who's going to come and attack you for that?"],[1942.8,"Verse 14, but even if you should suffer for righteousness' sake, you are blessed."],[1948.24,"And do not be afraid of their threats nor be troubled."],[1951.92,"According from Isaiah 8."],[1956.08,"So he says, but even if you should, even if that does happen, and of course that happened to Christ."],[1960.58,"That does happen."],[1962.31,"But it's less common."],[1967.79,"Then you are still blessed."],[1971.31,"Because verse 15, but sanctify the Lord God in your hearts, and always be ready to give a defense to everyone who asks you a reason for the hope that is in you with meekness and fear."],[1983.83,"Fear again. Having a good conscience, that when they defame you as evildoers, those who revile your good conduct in Christ may be ashamed."],[1995.43,"For it is better if it is the will of God to suffer for doing good than for doing evil."],[2002.23,"So that butt should probably be an and, but anyway."],[2010.55,"Sanctifying the Lord God in your hearts."],[2013.53,"Means treating him as holy."],[2016.14,"And so you say, be ready."],[2017.7,"The the word defense there is uh apologia."],[2022.87,"And which of course doesn't mean make an apology."],[2024.95,"It's more like apologetic."],[2026.25,"But the idea is not give a defense like be defensive."],[2029.86,"It's not saying like counterattack, but give an explanation."],[2034.63,"So this is presenting a situation where someone is attacking you or criticizing you, for doing good, meaning for following Christ."],[2042.69,"You're living your life in a way that's following Christ."],[2045.43,"And someone is making accusations against you and slandering you."],[2048.81,"So it's not saying defend yourself."],[2051.02,"It's saying be able to explain, hey, here's why I live my life this way."],[2057.02,"Here's why I do what I do."],[2058.4,"Here's why I am I am, doing this."],[2059.12,"And you do that with meekness"],[2062.62,"And again, fear is reverence."],[2064.96,"Meekness being the opposite of pride."],[2068.5,"You don't do this in arrogance."],[2070.42,"Doesn't mean you start with you idiot."],[2072.83,"And then heathen, you know, and and uh but um to explain that with having a good conscience, meaning, meaning that they actually are accusing you falsely."],[2090.34,"So this isn't saying, you know, you did something sinful, you're getting called out on it."],[2097.38,"Defend yourself. Quite the opposite."],[2100.36,"So he's saying having a good conscience, meaning that you honestly have a clean conscience before God"],[2108.31,"So that, again, so that this is the purpose, so that when they defame you as evildoers, when they come and hurl these accusations against you, and you have a clear conscience and you're able to explain why."],[2120.88,"You live the way you do, then those who are reviling your good conduct in Christ, those who are criticizing you as followers of Christ, will be ashamed."],[2130.28,"We'll be put to shame."],[2131.16,"The'yll be embarrassed."],[2132.52,"They'll look bad."],[2134.22,"Because of who you are that they're attacking."],[2140.3,"So he says it's better if it's the will of God to suffer for for doing good than doing evil."],[2142.62,"That's a basic truism"],[2147.53,"Because when we suffer for doing bad."],[2150.27,"You try and shoplift and you get caught."],[2152.27,"You can't be like, oh, why am I suffering?"],[2154.83,"Why God? Well, because you stole something."],[2157.64,"That's why. You know why."],[2160.78,"But suffering for doing good is a different kind of case."],[2165.36,"Think about the wise thief who's being crucified next to Christ."],[2169.2,"Remember when the other thieves started making fun of him?"],[2173.14,"The wise thief St."],[2174.26,"Dismas said, you know, you and I have this coming."],[2178.48,"But he didn't do anything."],[2180.17,"He's innocent of anything."],[2186.13,"Verse 18, for Christ also suffered once for sins, the just for the unjust, that he might bring us to God, being put to death in the flesh, but made alive by the Spirit."],[2200.37,"So this is Christ is being used as the example of this."],[2208.15,"It may be in your life, he's saying, if it's God's will, that you end up suffering for doing good."],[2214.23,"That even though you're doing what is right and trying to follow Christ, you're following that advice from the psalm he just gave, the best of your ability, you still end up suffering things."],[2224.23,"He says, Well, hey, Christ was perfect and sinless, and he suffered."],[2233.01,"And even though he was just and righteous, he suffered for us who are unjust and unrighteous."],[2241.14,"So that he could bring us to God."],[2244.76,"Not so that it didn't matter that we were unrighteous and unjust, but so that we could be brought back to God."],[2255.48,"And Christ then was put to death in the flesh, but made alive by the Spirit, the Holy Spirit."],[2262.12,"That's actually a good translation."],[2263.72,"Thank you, NKJV."],[2265.8,"I got to say that because I haven't got to say that much the way through the New Testament."],[2270.36,"But um sometimes this is translated being put to death of the flesh and made alive in the spirit"],[2277.84,"Without spirit being capitalized, even."],[2281.2,"As if like Christ's body died, but his spirit lives on, or something, which is not what he's saying."],[2293.08,"So now we get to another non controversial, totally straightforward part of St."],[2299.16,"Peter's Epistle. By whom that would be the Spirit, the Holy Spirit."],[2307.08,"He also went and preached to the spirits in prison."],[2312.36,"Okay, so this is in his death."],[2315.5,"He dies. Through his death, he goes and preaches to spirits that are in prison."],[2320.62,"Which ones? Verse 20, who formerly were disobedient, when once the divine long suffering waited in the days of Noah, while the ark was being prepared, in which a few, that is, eight souls, were saved through water."],[2336.14,"Okay, so that's straightforward."],[2337.68,"Like there's nothing."],[2339.97,"So St. Peter is saying, for a reason we'll get to here in a minute."],[2347.22,"Um, that during Christ's, between Christ's death and resurrection, he went to a prison for spirits."],[2356.14,"We have an eye cut of this"],[2358.9,"He went to a prison of spirits, and he preached to the spirits there."],[2364.82,"And he specifically preached or proclaimed, the verb can mean either of those, to some spirits who are disobedient in the days of Noah, specifically while the Ark was being built."],[2382.34,"Okay. So this passage rather infamously just completely flummoxed St."],[2388.5,"Augustine. It's the one passage where he's really like like, you read his commentary and, he's like, I've I've got nothing."],[2396.68,"So he kind of interpreted the Christ going and preaching thing as, well, I guess maybe like he wanted to take it as, well, maybe like he goes to the saints of the Old Testament in Sheol or Hades, and he preaches to them so that they could find salvation."],[2416.32,"But then he's like, But these are souls who sinned, so that would not be them."],[2422.96,"And then he's like, and I don't know why it would be just these ones from Noah's day."],[2428.66,"Like, why do they get special preaching?"],[2434.79,"So uh Saint Peter here is alluding to something."],[2442.23,"Um, something that is not in the Old Testament, unless. you're Ethiopian"],[2447.71,"That it is in your Old Testament, but it's giant related."],[2452.83,"Yes. Are you getting excited because I'm talking about giants?"],[2458.67,"So he's alluding to this."],[2463.55,"And I'm going to broach this now because this is going to come up again."],[2467.63,"Even more explicitly in 2 Peter."],[2470.27,"And then even more explicitly in St."],[2472.43,"Jude's Epistle. So these three epistles, um, all of which, back in the introduction to those three epistles, remember we talked about how these are all sort of products of the church in Rome uh in in the Apostolic era."],[2487.05,"Um so I I'm coming to the book of Enoch here is where we're going."],[2494.33,"So Saint Jude literally quotes it in his epistle."],[2499.29,"In the parallel section, we talked about how St."],[2501.53,"Jude's epistle is basically reproduced in Second Peter."],[2504.97,"Um it's strong it the quote isn't there in Second Peter, but there's an obvious allusion to the book of Enoch there."],[2513.13,"This is a less obvious allusion to the same section of the same text."],[2519.13,"Okay. So as I said, unless you're Ethiopian, it's not in the Bible."],[2523.38,"But we've seen along the way, we saw this with St."],[2526.34,"Paul, that New Testament authors often refer to and cite things that aren't part of the Bible."],[2533.86,"So in 1 Corinthians, we saw St"],[2538.06,"Paul quotes Menander, who was a comedic playwright in classical Greece."],[2547.02,"Only guy to ever win the Athenian prize twice."],[2550.3,"But definitely not part of the Old Testament."],[2553.04,"He was a pagan Greek playwright who wrote comedies."],[2556.48,"It wasn't even tragedies, it wasn't even serious."],[2560.72,"But he quotes him."],[2563.25,"To the Corinthians."],[2564.29,"Why? Well, because he's famous in Corinth."],[2566.79,"It's like quoting Shakespeare to an English speaker."],[2570.33,"Like, it's a it's a famous quote."],[2573.14,"And we saw how St."],[2574.1,"Paul referred to Janice and Jambras, where they contested with Moses."],[2576.74,"Well, those names are nowhere in the book of Exodus"],[2581.24,"But if you look at Second Temple Jewish literature, from around 200 BC, there's a whole bunch of texts talking about Janus and Jambras."],[2590.62,"That's always the names of the Egyptian magicians in all those texts."],[2595.36,"And so St."],[2596.0,"Paul just refers to that because the people he's talking to all know about that."],[2600.42,"So he could just refer to it."],[2601.78,"And we talked about the rock following Israel in the wilderness, how that came from."],[2605.94,"So the fact that they could even St."],[2608.98,"Jude quoting the book of Enoch does not make the Book of Enoch part of the Bible any more than Menander is part of the Bible."],[2616.46,"Um in Acts also we, talk about. one time St"],[2620.1,"Paul quoted a hymn to Zeus to the Athenians."],[2624.48,"So that's not part of the Bible either."],[2627.17,"Um, but this is this is a story with which everyone is familiar."],[2634.93,"Okay, with which everyone is familiar."],[2638.37,"How familiar? Well, the book of Enoch uh was wildly popular in Jewish communities at this time in history."],[2647.97,"To give you an example for the Dead Sea Scrolls, we have like their whole library."],[2652.07,"Because they hid all their scrolls."],[2653.83,"So when you look at a count of how many copies they had of different books, number one is Genesis."],[2660.63,"Number two is the book of Enoch."],[2662.63,"Exodus is like fourth"],[2667.71,"All the other books of the Old Testament are further down."],[2671.39,"So that's how popular the Book of Enoch is."],[2673.39,"It's just wildly popular everywhere."],[2675.55,"The church fathers were still reading it, even though the vast majority of them did not think it was part of the Old Testament."],[2681.87,"They weren't reading it in their churches, they were reading it at home."],[2687.39,"So this is just a well known Jewish text."],[2693.71,"Now, in this text, there's a group of spirits who sinned in the days of Noah."],[2702.05,"It's in the first part of the book of Enoch that's called the Book of the Watchers"],[2707.29,"And the Watchers, they're called watchers because it's an idea like guardian angels."],[2712.15,"These are angels who were bad."],[2714.52,"Specifically, they they taught sorcery to uh in the book of Enoch to human women so that these human women could become pregnant with these sort of demonized babies."],[2727.56,"That's the Nephilim, the giants."],[2729.66,"That's where they come from."],[2731.02,"Who are mentioned in Genesis 6 1 through 4."],[2734.48,"And so, as a punishment for this, for having done this, and having revealed not just sorcery, but all kinds of forbidden knowledge to Cain's line in particular."],[2747.16,"Uh, they get imprisoned. in the abyss, by um by God in the book of Enoch"],[2755.37,"And this is a version of a story that's common all through the ancient world, that Genesis 6, 1 through 4 is probably alluding to."],[2763.87,"So this is in the Mesopotamian version of the story, these spirits are the Abkalu."],[2768.91,"They go and teach wisdom to the kings, before the flood."],[2774.13,"They get punished by the gods and imprisoned in in the abyss beneath the river of Euphrates."],[2781.01,"And in the king list, there's an Abkalu, who's a teacher for each king before the flood, and then after the flood, all the kings are part human, part Abkalu."],[2791.51,"This like giant idea."],[2793.39,"Um, the Greek version of this is like Prometheus."],[2796.93,"He comes and gives fire to man."],[2799.5,"The man isn't supposed to have, so the gods punish him, by imprisoning him."],[2804.28,"Also the Titans being imprisoned in Tartarus."],[2807.88,"Uh Saint Peter, spoilers in 2 Peter is going to use the word Tartarus when referring to these same."],[2815.13,"Um so this is just this common ancient idea, and the Jewish version, sort of full Jewish version of this story, is what you find in the book of Enoch."],[2825.05,"Now, in the book of Enoch, these these angels who sinned in the days of Noah, who are imprisoned, there's a part of the story where Enoch in the Book of the Watchers goes on. sort of this tour of the cosmos"],[2838.85,"So he goes and he sees the ends of the earth, then he goes and he sees the heavens and the angels and everything."],[2845.09,"The archangels, the seven archangels take him on this tour."],[2847.89,"Then he goes, he sees paradise, he goes into the abyss."],[2851.83,"Or he goes into Sheol or Hades."],[2854.57,"Sees the different parts of Hades, sees all the things."],[2858.01,"And he goes to where these angels are imprisoned."],[2861.13,"And the angels sort of say to Enoch, hey, uh, you got a you got a good relationship with God."],[2867.29,"Uh, can you can you go put in a good word for us?"],[2869.69,"Maybe get us out of here."],[2871.3,"They're not right, they don't actually repent."],[2873.78,"They're not like, we're sorry we did this."],[2875.78,"They just don't like being imprisoned."],[2878.02,"They're like, hey, maybe you can get us let out."],[2880.68,"So Enoch, when he's back in heaven, sort of asks, hey, is there any way?"],[2885.96,"He kind of tries to intercede for them."],[2887.74,"Say, hey, is there any way, God, you could have mercy on these angels?"],[2890.78,"And God says, basically, no."],[2893.34,"In fact, go back down there and tell them they're doomed."],[2897.76,"And it's all over for them, and there's no mercy for them."],[2900.88,"And so Enoch goes down back down to where they are and tells them that."],[2906.98,"And tells them that."],[2908.9,"Um, I am positing that this is what St."],[2916.5,"Peter is referring to, and making a connection with Christ's heroine of Hades, Christ going to Hades after his death and before his resurrection, that he is there, because notice it doesn't say preaching the gospel."],[2936.55,"Just says preaching or proclaiming something to them."],[2941.56,"That this is actually him proclaiming their doom to them because they've been defeated, by his death and resurrection."],[2949.92,"And the people they afflicted have been set free."],[2953.12,"Or in context"],[2953.4,"Why is that a good interpretation of this?"],[2956.04,"Well, because it makes sense in contrast."],[2960.08,"Sorry, in context."],[2961.76,"Because what is Saint Peter using this as an example of?"],[2964.64,"example of? This is the ultimate what"],[2969.88,"Before the word for."],[2973.24,"This is the ultimate example of someone suffering unjustly, Christ, and then his accusers being put to shame."],[2984.82,"That's what St."],[2985.54,"Peter was just talking about."],[2989.11,"That if you're falsely accused and you suffer for righteousness' sake, your accusers will be put to shame."],[2996.31,"Here's the ultimate example."],[2998.63,"Christ suffered for righteousness."],[3000.57,"He was put to death."],[3001.61,"He was murdered."],[3002.41,"He was killed."],[3003.87,"But not only was he vindicated by being made alive by the Holy Spirit, but his enemies were put to shame."],[3014.47,"Remember what St."],[3015.27,"Paul said in Colossians?"],[3017.85,"That Christ had on the cross made a public spectacle of the powers and principalities."],[3024.87,"It's the same kind of idea that St."],[3027.51,"Peter's getting at, getting at here."],[3032.47,"And there are other explanations of this, but I don't know of another explanation of this that again makes sense in context, in terms of why this would be an example of Christians being vindicated who suffer unjustly."],[3047.11,"Because point one is they're going to be resurrected like Christ."],[3051.12,"But then point two was about the accusers being put to shame."],[3063.16,"And so um he then shifts."],[3066.86,"Because he has Noah."],[3068.14,"Now that we're talking about Noah, he says, remember in those days, there were only eight souls."],[3073.99,"Noah and his wife, and his sons and their wives."],[3077.31,"Eight lives who are saved through water."],[3082.27,"Verse 21, there is also an anti-type which now saves us, baptism."],[3090.99,"So he's now making a comparison between. the flood, Noah's flood, and baptism"],[3098.87,"Not the removal of the filth of the flesh, but the answer of a good conscience toward God."],[3105.83,"He's still talking about that good conscience thing."],[3108.36,"That he brought up earlier."],[3112.52,"Through the resurrection of Jesus Christ."],[3124.84,"In the flood, the world gets cleansed."],[3128.01,"All the evil and wickedness in the world."],[3131.15,"That Noah suffered through."],[3132.99,"We're gonna go come back to all this in 2 Peter and in Jude."],[3136.19,"All of this is gonna recur."],[3137.91,"All this has happened before, and all this will happen again."],[3140.49,"But um so he's saying, you know, not the not clean the filth of the flesh."],[3146.5,"Baptism isn't like taking a bath."],[3148.6,"Where you just wash yourself off."],[3151.21,"But the evil is purged out of the world."],[3154.27,"And so now Noah and his family are preserved, not only preserved through that, but are saved through that, saved from that world that existed before the flood and its hostility."],[3165.17,"Because remember, he's talking about Christians in this hostile accusing world."],[3169.97,"Remember, everybody laughed and mocked Noah because he's out there building a boat."],[3174.57,"So, so it's salvation from those accusers, from those hostile forces."],[3181.87,"So, baptism is an anti type of that because it's sort of a cutting off, of us from the world."],[3189.19,"And that's why he talks about the cleaning of our conscience."],[3196.79,"So you have a good conscience toward God through the resurrection of Jesus Christ, which is also what happens in baptism."],[3204.23,"As many of you as have been baptized into Christ have put on Christ."],[3207.61,"We're buried with him in baptism and rise again right out of the water."],[3213.93,"Verse 22: who has gone into heaven and is at the right hand of God."],[3219.05,"Angels and authorities and powers having been made subject to him."],[3226.3,"So all of those powers, all those hostile powers out there in the world that are animating the Roman Empire, that are animating the evil and the accusations and the persecution directed at Christians, Christ has already defeated them."],[3241.42,"He already told them we're they're doomed right before he rose."],[3246.35,"He set us free from them."],[3248.53,"They're dead to us."],[3250.15,"And he's now sitting at the right hand of God ruling over the world, and they're under his feet."],[3256.95,"Listen next time as Father Stephen DeYoung continues his study of the scriptures on the Whole Counsel of God."],[3264.15,"Father. Stephen's email address is wholecounsel at ancientfaith.com"],[3269.63,"That's wholecounsel at ancientfaith.com."]],"o":[{"t":21.2,"lv":2,"h":"Recap: Peter's \"therefore\" pattern","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Picking up at <b>1 Peter 3:1</b>; brief recap of ch. 2 first</li><li class=\"d0\">Peter uses <b>\"therefore\"</b> like St. Paul - lay something out, then apply</li><li class=\"d1\">Paul: <b>whole first part</b> theology, then \"therefore\" and practical to the end</li><li class=\"d1\">Peter <b>switches back and forth</b> - lay out, apply, lay out, apply</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>chain of linked arguments</b>, not \"one big whole\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Chiefly lately: calling of the church as a <b>priesthood</b>, tied to OT priesthood</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>holiness = set apart</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Israel set apart from nations, priesthood set apart from Israel - now <b>required of Christians</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Church <b>internal</b> relations: holy, pure, <b>unity of love</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Now shifted: how the community relates to the <b>world outside</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Ended last time on <b>slavery</b> - \"super non-controversial\"</li></ul>"},{"t":135.65,"lv":3,"h":"Persecution from outside: do good","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Frame: <b>persecution and derision</b> from outside toward Christians</li><li class=\"d0\">Right response: <b>not fight back</b>, not even defend ourselves or the church → <b>do good</b></li><li class=\"d1\">so false accusations make them <b>look foolish</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Christians known for <b>kindness</b>, being <b>Christ-like</b> to outsiders</li><li class=\"d0\">Even if it fails in this life: at <b>day of judgment</b> no accusation will stick</li><li class=\"d0\">Then <b>specific difficult cases</b></li><li class=\"d0\">First: <b>slaves</b></li><li class=\"d1\">slaves and women = <b>most common converts</b> in 1st c. and most of 2nd</li><li class=\"d1\">even <b>bishops who were slaves</b> - icon of Christ, leader outside church, yet someone \"owned\" them</li><li class=\"d1\">same principle: <b>no slave revolts</b>, no killing the master</li><li class=\"d1\">do good → master possibly <b>shamed</b>; barring that, <b>rewarded before Christ</b></li></ul>"},{"t":381.59,"lv":2,"h":"Don't read it out of context","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Why lay this out first: easy to pull husbands/wives stuff out of context</li><li class=\"d1\">\"Peter weighing in on <b>gender theory</b>\" - <b>not what this is</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Peril of the <b>concordance way</b> of reading the Bible</li><li class=\"d1\">\"I want to find out about faith\" → gather every verse → <b>\"violence to the text\"</b></li><li class=\"d1\">same with <b>men and women</b>: pull all out, construct something</li><li class=\"d0\">Wouldn't read any other book that way</li><li class=\"d1\">German history, find all the places about <b>sausage</b> → \"what the author has to say about sausage\"</li><li class=\"d1\"><i>Moby Dick</i> and the <b>harpoons</b> - only for a dissertation or journal article</li><li class=\"d0\">Peter does speak to husbands and wives, like he did to slaves and owners</li><li class=\"d1\">but as <b>examples of a general principle</b></li><li class=\"d1\">miss the principle, don't compare the examples → <b>can't interpret correctly</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Asks for questions, then reads</li></ul>"},{"t":532.7,"lv":2,"h":"1 Peter 3:1-2 - Wives, likewise","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Reads <b>3:1-2</b>: be submissive to your own husbands; won <b>\"without a word\"</b> by chaste conduct with fear</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>\"Likewise\"</b> = literally Greek <b>\"in the same way\"</b></li><li class=\"d1\">as what? <b>as the slaves</b> - that's the comparison</li><li class=\"d0\">Why? Writing to <b>women in the Roman world</b> - not much difference in ability to <b>self-govern</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Roman husband, esp. a citizen, could <b>have her killed</b> if displeased</li><li class=\"d1\">or <b>divorce</b> her and throw her on the street</li><li class=\"d1\">\"he could do whatever he wants\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Arranged marriages</b>, early teens - women didn't pick</li><li class=\"d1\">could end up with someone \"truly horrible\" - <b>nothing they could do</b></li><li class=\"d1\">could only <b>run away</b> like a slave; no divorce and remarry</li><li class=\"d1\">\"likewise\" powerless, depending on the man</li></ul>"},{"t":694.71,"lv":3,"h":"\"Your own\" husbands; who is not obeying the Word","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>\"Your own husbands\"</b> - not all women to all men</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>one particular relationship</b>: how a Christian woman conducts herself in it</li><li class=\"d0\">\"Do not obey the <b>word</b>\" - should be <b>capital W</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not the Bible - <b>they don't have a Bible</b></li><li class=\"d1\">= husband <b>not a Christian</b>, a pagan</li><li class=\"d0\">Slaves and women = most common converts → good reason</li><li class=\"d1\">Christianity the only religion or philosophy saying they were <b>equally human</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not equal rights or voting - <b>human like Caesar is human</b></li><li class=\"d1\">attractive to people told they were <b>subhuman</b>, treated like animals</li><li class=\"d0\">Greeks \"especially bad\" - \"rabid misogynists\"</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Aristotle</b>: women a <b>birth defect</b>, fetuses that \"didn't quite gel\"</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Socrates</b>: man can't love a woman, she's <b>beneath him</b> - \"and Socrates was married\"</li></ul>"},{"t":850.12,"lv":3,"h":"Pagan husbands and the goal of winning them","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Not hypothetical - <b>many Christian women</b> married to pagan Roman men</li><li class=\"d0\">Pagan Romans didn't value <b>fruit of the Spirit</b> - kindness, gentleness, faithfulness, self-control</li><li class=\"d1\">Roman society cultivated the <b>opposite</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><i>Pax Romana</i> - \"made a wasteland and called it peace\"</li><li class=\"d1\">valued <b>power, strength, swift action</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Nonetheless: <b>be obedient</b>, don't flee, keep <b>peace in the household</b></li><li class=\"d1\">same as slaves not running away</li><li class=\"d0\">Why? Not because women \"should just take what they can get\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>So that</b> they may be <b>won without a word</b> by conduct</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>\"fear\"</b> = reverence, <b>fear of God</b> - not terror of the husband</li><li class=\"d0\">Goal: <b>not freedom</b>, not hoping he's widowed, not \"try again\"</li><li class=\"d1\">goal = <b>his salvation</b>; if he finds it, <b>dynamics change</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1017.38,"lv":2,"h":"1 Peter 3:3-6 - Inner adornment, Sarah","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>3:3-4</b>: not outward adornment - hair, <b>gold</b>, fine apparel</li><li class=\"d1\">rather the <b>hidden person of the heart</b>, <b>incorruptible beauty</b>, gentle and quiet spirit</li><li class=\"d0\">Comparison: not outward, but <b>virtue</b></li><li class=\"d1\">becoming like Christ, serving Christ</li><li class=\"d1\">that's what <b>wins him over</b> - \"not him looking at you and thinking you're really pretty\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>3:5-6</b>: holy women who trusted God, <b>Sarah obeyed Abraham</b>, \"calling him Lord\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Earlier \"with fear\" - now \"<b>not afraid with any terror</b>\" → fear = reverence, \"kind of a bad translation\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Requires <b>bravery, courage, endurance, patience</b> with a <b>petty tyrant</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Why <b>Abraham and Sarah</b>: in between</li><li class=\"d1\">lied she was his wife, men tried to take her - <b>Egypt twice</b>; <b>Hagar</b> episode</li><li class=\"d1\">not <b>Isaac and Rebecca</b> - \"perfect and beautiful loving\"</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Jacob, Leah, Rachel</b> - \"all kinds of problematic\"</li><li class=\"d1\">A and S \"a little bit rocky\" but <b>loving ultimately</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1170.01,"lv":3,"h":"Wives addressed as Christians and as humans","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Moderns \"sidetracked by the word <b>submissive</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Look at <b>how he talks to her</b> - encouraging her <b>as a human</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Earlier in epistle: addressed to Christians generally - <b>men too</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"gird up the loins of your mind,\" be <b>sober</b>, give up former lusts</li><li class=\"d1\">so he treats pagans' wives as <b>Christians, as humans</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Marked change from the old covenant</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Torah commandments: <b>almost none directed at women</b></li><li class=\"d1\">no \"do not covet your neighbor's wife <b>or husband</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">sexual immorality laws all to <b>men</b> - incl. homosexuality, \"only directed at men\"</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Late Bronze Age</b>: women didn't make those decisions → <b>not held accountable</b></li><li class=\"d1\">men held accountable as the decision-makers</li></ul>"},{"t":1277.56,"lv":2,"h":"The shift in the early church","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Something has <b>shifted</b> - women in early church have <b>more autonomy</b> (\"not as much as today\")</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Paul</b>: infamous <b>\"women silent in church\"</b> passage</li><li class=\"d1\">word for silence = the one used for <b>meditation</b></li><li class=\"d1\">before: women \"too dumb to learn\" - men hear the synagogue sermon, women go have <b>coffee-time chat</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Paul: they need to listen, <b>become disciples</b> too</li><li class=\"d0\">Peter doing the <b>same</b></li><li class=\"d1\">could just tell men to be nicer - valid; <b>Paul</b>: \"husbands love your wives as Christ loves the church\"</li><li class=\"d1\">but he's <b>also speaking to the wives</b>, in a hard situation</li><li class=\"d0\">Message: you can acquire <b>these virtues</b> as much as a man</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>self-controlled, patient, loving, compassionate, courageous</b></li><li class=\"d1\">can → <b>called to</b> → <b>now responsible</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1386.66,"lv":2,"h":"1 Peter 3:7 - Husbands: the \"weaker vessel\"","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Verse 7: how many verses to husbands? <b>One</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Reads: dwell with understanding, honor wife as <b>weaker vessel</b>, <b>heirs together</b>, prayers not hindered</li><li class=\"d0\">\"How dare you, sir?\" - audience comment: if weaker, <b>comparable to a weak vessel</b> - both weak, <b>equal</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Also the <b>vessel</b> part matters</li><li class=\"d1\">not \"physically weaker\" - yes on average males stronger, <b>not the point</b></li><li class=\"d1\">vessel = <b>pottery</b>; weaker = <b>delicate, fragile</b>, not \"can't lift as much\"</li><li class=\"d1\">carrying <b>glass</b>: not rough, <b>gentle</b>, <b>care</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Context: husbands as <b>petty tyrants</b>, treating wife like a <b>slave</b></li><li class=\"d1\">opposite = <b>gentleness, respect, kindness, care</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not brutal - \"the exact opposite\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1555.91,"lv":3,"h":"Co-heirs; prayers not hindered","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>\"Heirs together of the grace of life\"</b> - co-heirs</li><li class=\"d0\">Why powerful as equality: <b>women generally not heirs</b></li><li class=\"d1\">son, the male, was the heir</li><li class=\"d1\">Peter: <b>both heirs of salvation</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Not about <b>physical strength</b> or <b>roles in society</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>before Christ</b>: Paul's \"no male or female, Jew or Gentile, slave or free\"</li><li class=\"d1\">same plane for salvation</li><li class=\"d0\">So: don't treat her as <b>slave, subhuman</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>hold her up</b> as precious, delicate, cared for</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>\"So that your prayers may not be hindered\"</b></li><li class=\"d1\">ask God for mercy - how, if not <b>merciful to your own wife</b>?</li><li class=\"d1\">kindness and compassion to <b>the closest person</b></li><li class=\"d1\">else don't expect God to treat you as you don't treat her</li><li class=\"d0\">She is legally helpless before husband; <b>we're helpless before God</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>forgive to be forgiven</b> - principle through the gospels</li><li class=\"d1\">prayers go \"no further than the <b>ceiling</b>\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1730.27,"lv":2,"h":"1 Peter 3:8-9 - Finally: one mind","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>\"Finally\"</b> - Paul did this too; \"dead center\" of the epistle</li><li class=\"d1\">like \"let us complete our prayer to the Lord\" with <b>45 minutes left in liturgy</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Reads: <b>one mind, compassion, love as brothers</b>, tenderhearted, courteous</li><li class=\"d0\">Extends husband/wife to <b>all in the church</b></li><li class=\"d1\">how a <b>marriage</b> should be = how the <b>whole community</b> behaves</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Not returning evil for evil</b> or reviling for reviling - <b>blessing</b> instead</li><li class=\"d1\">called to this to <b>inherit a blessing</b></li><li class=\"d0\">People even in the church will <b>do evil</b> and say evil to you</li><li class=\"d1\">choice: <b>hit back</b> or <b>bless</b></li><li class=\"d0\">We expect to inherit blessing → <b>bless them</b> even if they don't bless us</li><li class=\"d0\">What we want from God we <b>give to others</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"we're not always that great toward God\" and still want blessing</li></ul>"},{"t":1843.33,"lv":2,"h":"1 Peter 3:10-14 - Psalm 34 and suffering for righteousness","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>3:10-12</b> = <b>Psalm 34</b> (<b>33 in the Greek</b>), quoted at length</li><li class=\"d0\">Ties together what he's been saying - applied to specific cases</li><li class=\"d1\">love life, <b>good days</b>: tongue from evil, lips from deceit</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>turn from evil, do good</b> - \"<b>repent</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>seek peace and pursue it</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>eyes of the Lord</b> on righteous, ears open to prayers</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>face of the Lord against</b> those who do evil</li><li class=\"d0\">\"Almost like <b>works are important</b>. But I digress.\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>3:13</b>: \"who will harm you?\" - segue</li><li class=\"d1\">pursuing peace and good, speaking truth → who attacks you for that?</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>3:14</b>: <b>even if you suffer</b> for righteousness, <b>blessed</b>; not afraid of threats - from <b>Isaiah 8</b></li><li class=\"d1\">it happened to Christ; <b>less common</b></li><li class=\"d1\">still blessed</li></ul>"},{"t":1971.31,"lv":2,"h":"1 Peter 3:15-17 - Always ready to give an answer","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>3:15</b>: <b>sanctify the Lord God</b> in hearts = treat him as <b>holy</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Be ready to give a <b>defense</b> - <i>apologia</i></li><li class=\"d1\">not \"make an apology\" - closer to <b>apologetic</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>not</b> \"be defensive,\" not counterattack → <b>give an explanation</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Situation: attacked for <b>doing good = following Christ</b>, slandered</li><li class=\"d1\">not \"defend yourself\" - explain <b>\"here's why I live this way\"</b></li><li class=\"d0\">With <b>meekness</b> (opposite of <b>pride</b>, not arrogance) and <b>fear</b> = reverence</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Good conscience</b>: accusations are <b>false</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>not</b> \"you did something sinful, defend yourself\" - \"quite the opposite\"</li><li class=\"d1\">honestly <b>clean conscience before God</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Purpose: those who defame you, revile your good conduct in Christ → <b>ashamed</b></li><li class=\"d1\">embarrassed, \"look bad\" because of who they attack</li><li class=\"d0\">Note: \"but\" in the text \"should probably be an <b>and</b>\"</li></ul>"},{"t":2140.3,"lv":2,"h":"1 Peter 3:17-18 - Better to suffer for doing good","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>3:17</b>: better to suffer for doing <b>good</b> than <b>evil</b> - <b>\"basic truism\"</b></li><li class=\"d1\">shoplift and get caught - \"why God?\" - <b>because you stole</b></li><li class=\"d1\">suffering for good = \"different kind of case\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Wise thief</b> beside Christ - St. <b>Dismas</b></li><li class=\"d1\">other thief mocked; Dismas: \"you and I have <b>this coming</b>\"; <b>he didn't do anything</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>3:18</b>: Christ suffered <b>once</b> for sins, <b>just for the unjust</b>, to bring us to God</li><li class=\"d1\">Christ = the <b>example</b>: you may suffer even if following the Psalm \"to the best of your ability\"</li><li class=\"d1\">Christ <b>perfect, sinless</b>, still suffered</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>not</b> \"it didn't matter we were unrighteous\" - so we could be <b>brought back to God</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Put to death in the flesh, made alive by the Spirit</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Holy Spirit</b> - \"good translation, thank you, NKJV\"</li><li class=\"d1\">some versions: \"of the flesh, in the spirit\" lowercase → \"<b>body died, spirit lives on</b>\" - not what he says</li></ul>"},{"t":2293.08,"lv":2,"h":"1 Peter 3:19-20 - Preaching to spirits in prison","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">\"Another non-controversial, totally straightforward part\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>3:19</b>: by the Spirit, he <b>went and preached to the spirits in prison</b></li><li class=\"d1\">in <b>his death</b>; who? <b>3:20</b>: disobedient in <b>days of Noah</b>, ark being prepared, <b>eight souls</b> saved through water</li><li class=\"d0\">Between Christ's <b>death and resurrection</b>: went to a <b>prison for spirits</b> - <b>icon of this</b></li><li class=\"d1\">verb = <b>preached or proclaimed</b>, either</li><li class=\"d1\">specifically to spirits <b>disobedient while the Ark was built</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>St. Augustine</b> - passage \"completely flummoxed\" him: \"I've got nothing\"</li><li class=\"d1\">maybe Christ went to <b>OT saints in Sheol/Hades</b> to preach salvation</li><li class=\"d1\">but these are <b>souls who sinned</b> - wouldn't be them</li><li class=\"d1\">and why <b>only Noah's day</b>? why \"special preaching\"?</li></ul>"},{"t":2434.79,"lv":2,"h":"Peter alludes to the Book of Enoch","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Peter alluding to something <b>not in the OT</b> - \"unless you're <b>Ethiopian</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>giant</b> related - \"getting excited because I'm talking about giants?\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Broaching now: comes up <b>more explicitly in 2 Peter</b>, then <b>Jude</b></li><li class=\"d1\">three epistles = products of the <b>church in Rome</b>, apostolic era</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Book of Enoch</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Jude quotes it</b> directly</li><li class=\"d1\">2 Peter (= Jude reproduced): <b>obvious allusion</b>, no quote</li><li class=\"d1\">here: <b>less obvious allusion</b> to the same section</li><li class=\"d0\">Not in the Bible - but <b>NT authors cite non-Bible things</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2519.13,"lv":3,"h":"Non-biblical sources in the NT; Enoch's popularity","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Paul in <b>1 Corinthians</b> quotes <b>Menander</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>pagan Greek comedic playwright</b> - not even tragedies, \"not even serious\"</li><li class=\"d1\">famous in Corinth - \"like quoting <b>Shakespeare</b> to an English speaker\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul on <b>Jannes and Jambres</b>, who contested Moses</li><li class=\"d1\">names <b>nowhere in Exodus</b>; Second Temple literature from ~<b>200 BC</b> - always the Egyptian magicians</li><li class=\"d1\">readers all knew it</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Rock following Israel</b> in the wilderness - same</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Acts</b>: Paul quoted a <b>hymn to Zeus</b> to the Athenians</li><li class=\"d0\">Jude quoting Enoch <b>no more makes it Bible</b> than Menander</li><li class=\"d0\">Enoch <b>wildly popular</b> in Jewish communities</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Dead Sea Scrolls</b> copies: #1 <b>Genesis</b>, #2 <b>Enoch</b>, Exodus ~4th</li><li class=\"d1\">church fathers still read it - <b>at home, not in church</b>; most didn't count it as OT</li></ul>"},{"t":2693.71,"lv":3,"h":"The Book of the Watchers and ancient parallels","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">First part of Enoch = <b>Book of the Watchers</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Watchers: like <b>guardian angels</b>, but <b>bad</b></li><li class=\"d1\">taught <b>sorcery</b> to human women → <b>demonized babies</b> = <b>Nephilim, giants</b> - <b>Genesis 6:1-4</b></li><li class=\"d1\">also <b>forbidden knowledge</b> to <b>Cain's line</b></li><li class=\"d1\">punishment: <b>imprisoned in the abyss</b> by God</li><li class=\"d0\">Story common through the ancient world - Gen 6:1-4 probably alludes</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Mesopotamian</b>: <i>Apkalu</i> teach wisdom to kings <b>before the flood</b></li><li class=\"d1\">punished, imprisoned in abyss beneath the <b>Euphrates</b></li><li class=\"d1\">king list: an Apkalu teacher per king; after flood kings <b>part human, part Apkalu</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Greek</b>: <b>Prometheus</b> gives fire, imprisoned; <b>Titans in Tartarus</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Peter in <b>2 Peter</b> uses <b>Tartarus</b> for the same</li><li class=\"d0\">Enoch = \"full Jewish version\"</li></ul>"},{"t":2825.05,"lv":3,"h":"Enoch's tour and the Watchers' plea","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Enoch's <b>tour of the cosmos</b> in the Watchers</li><li class=\"d1\">ends of the earth, heavens and angels, led by the <b>seven archangels</b></li><li class=\"d1\">paradise; <b>abyss / Sheol / Hades</b>, its different parts</li><li class=\"d0\">Reaches where the angels are <b>imprisoned</b></li><li class=\"d1\">they say: \"you have a good relationship with God - <b>put in a good word</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>not repentant</b> - \"just don't like being imprisoned\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Enoch back in heaven <b>intercedes</b>: any mercy?</li><li class=\"d1\">God: <b>no</b> - \"go back down and <b>tell them they're doomed</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>no mercy</b>; Enoch goes back and tells them</li></ul>"},{"t":2908.9,"lv":2,"h":"Peter's meaning: Christ proclaims their doom","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">His proposal: Peter links this to <b>Christ's harrowing of Hades</b>, between death and resurrection</li><li class=\"d1\">text doesn't say <b>\"preaching the gospel\"</b> - just <b>proclaiming</b> something</li><li class=\"d1\">= proclaiming their <b>doom</b>, <b>defeated</b> by death and resurrection</li><li class=\"d1\">people they afflicted <b>set free</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Why a good reading: fits <b>context</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Peter was just on false accusation: suffer for righteousness → <b>accusers put to shame</b></li><li class=\"d1\">here the <b>ultimate example</b>: Christ murdered, yet <b>vindicated</b> by the Spirit and <b>enemies shamed</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Colossians</b>: on the cross a <b>public spectacle</b> of powers and principalities</li><li class=\"d0\">Other explanations exist - none that <b>makes sense in context</b> as vindication of Christians who suffer unjustly</li><li class=\"d0\">Two points: <b>resurrected like Christ</b>; <b>accusers put to shame</b></li></ul>"},{"t":3063.16,"lv":2,"h":"1 Peter 3:21 - Flood and baptism","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Noah: <b>eight souls</b> - Noah, wife, sons, their wives - saved <b>through water</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>3:21</b>: antitype that now saves us, <b>baptism</b></li><li class=\"d1\">comparison of <b>Noah's flood</b> and baptism</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>not</b> removal of filth of the flesh - \"not like taking a bath\"</li><li class=\"d1\">but the <b>answer of a good conscience</b> toward God, <b>through the resurrection of Jesus Christ</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Flood: world <b>cleansed</b>, evil purged</li><li class=\"d1\">\"all this has happened before\" - comes back in <b>2 Peter and Jude</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Noah's family <b>saved from that world</b> and its hostility</li><li class=\"d1\">everyone <b>laughed and mocked</b> Noah building a boat</li><li class=\"d0\">Salvation from <b>accusers, hostile forces</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Baptism = a <b>cutting off</b> from the world → <b>cleaning of conscience</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>put on Christ</b>; buried with him, <b>rise out of the water</b></li></ul>"},{"t":3213.93,"lv":3,"h":"1 Peter 3:22 - Christ at the right hand","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Gone into heaven, <b>right hand of God</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>angels, authorities, powers</b> made subject to him</li><li class=\"d0\">Hostile powers animating <b>Roman Empire</b>, the evil, accusations, <b>persecution</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Christ <b>already defeated</b> them</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>told them they're doomed</b> before he rose</li><li class=\"d1\">set us free - \"<b>they're dead to us</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Now <b>ruling over the world</b>, they're <b>under his feet</b></li></ul>"}]});
